e"},{"id":"B","text":"Is"},{"id":"C","text":"Am"},{"id":"D","text":"Are"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Place not after be. For a standard yes/no question, put be before the subject. Do not add do or does. Focus on **question** in this question.","explanation":"In a standard yes/no question, is goes before “the water”.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"question","contextKey":"be-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-be","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"fe465177fb847bb0883bc5f821dd50bd58b6661c0d5135847b98fd6dc8b833cc","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **question**. Place not after be. For a standard yes/no question, put be before the subject. Do not add do or does.","In a standard yes/no question, is goes before “the water”.","Choose **Is**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Base **be** does not supply the finite agreement required here. In a standard yes/no question, is goes before “the water”.","B":"In a standard yes/no question, is goes before “the water”.","C":"**am** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. In a standard yes/no question, is goes before “the water”.","D":"**are** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. In a standard yes/no question, is goes before “the water”."}}},{"id":"8dbb9f80-e35a-5c7f-aeca-bab433d77cc3","topicSlug":"present-simple-be","level":"A1","prompt":"Replace the incorrect verb: “The water are clear in this stream.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"be"},{"id":"B","text":"is"},{"id":"C","text":"am"},{"id":"D","text":"are"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Use am with I, is with a singular subject, and are with you or a plural subject. Be describes identity, location or a state. Focus on **correction** in this question.","explanation":"Replace are with is, which agrees with “the water”.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"correction","contextKey":"be-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-be","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"c1857bc16fccc5714559d06ad3ad8bbf351d914b39a0ea4be7377d94d8ca74d1","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **correction**. Use am with I, is with a singular subject, and are with you or a plural subject. Be describes identity, location or a state.","Replace are with is, which agrees with “the water”.","Choose **is**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Base **be** does not supply the finite agreement required here. Replace are with is, which agrees with “the water”.","B":"Replace are with is, which agrees with “the water”.","C":"**am** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Replace are with is, which agrees with “the water”.","D":"**are** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Replace are with is, which agrees with “the water”."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Be links a subject to an identity, a state or a location. It does not need another action verb to make a complete sentence: The room is quiet is already complete. First identify the subject, then select the form of be. English normally keeps this verb where some other languages would leave it out."],"rules":[{"title":"Present forms","explanation":"Use am with I, is with a singular subject, and are with you or a plural subject. Be describes identity, location or a state.","examples":["I am near the exit.","The keys are on a hook.","The books **are** heavy, but the bag **is** light."],"pattern":"**I am** · **he / she / it is** · **you / we / they are**","paragraphs":["A noun phrase can be longer than one word. In The keys on the table are mine, keys is the head and is plural. A phrase beginning with you takes are even when you are addressing only one person. A name such as Mia normally takes is."]},{"title":"Negatives and questions","explanation":"Place not after be. For a standard yes/no question, put be before the subject. Do not add do or does.","examples":["The door is not locked.","Are the seats free?","Where **are** my glasses? — They **are** on the desk."],"pattern":"Subject + **be + not** / **Be + subject**?","paragraphs":["Be makes its own negative and question. The statement She is ready becomes She is not ready and Is she ready? Do not borrow do from the present-simple action-verb pattern. A question word goes before the inverted verb: Where are the keys?"]},{"title":"Contractions","explanation":"In everyday English, I am becomes I’m and they are becomes they’re. Full forms remain correct. In positive short answers, keep the full verb.","examples":["Yes, we are.","No, it is not.","It **is not** cold. / It **isn’t** cold."],"pattern":"Yes, I **am**. / No, I **am not**.","paragraphs":["Contractions make speech and informal writing shorter: I’m, you’re, she’s, isn’t and aren’t. Full forms are still correct and may add emphasis. A positive short answer normally ends with a full auxiliary: Yes, she is, not Yes, she’s."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Be also appears as an auxiliary in continuous and passive constructions. Compare She is happy (a state), She is reading (an activity in progress), and The letter is signed (a passive or a resulting state). The words after be determine its role.","examples":["The books **are** heavy, but the bag **is** light.","Where **are** my glasses? — They **are** on the desk.","It **is not** cold. / It **isn’t** cold."]}],"recap":["**I am** · **he / she / it is** · **you / we / they are**","Subject + **be + not** / **Be + subject**?","Yes, I **am**. / No, I **am not**."]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["pronouns-and-possessive-determiners"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
